Give a repository a Tailscale exit node in Settings -> Repository variables,
and show it on the repository's card and in the new-session form.

The editor gets an "Exit node" switch after the setup script, with a
single-line field for the exit node's machine name, MagicDNS name or
Tailscale IP. Unlike a value or the SSH key it isn't secret, so it comes back
to be edited, like the instructions. Saving sends exit_node to
PUT /v1/repo-env (images/server/omnigent_repo_env_api.py): the name while
it's on, null to remove a stored one switched off, and nothing otherwise, so
the server keeps what it has. exitNodeError (lib/repoEnv.ts) checks the name
as the server does: 1-253 letters, digits, dots, dashes and colons. An exit
node alone is enough to save a repository.

The card lists "exit node" in its summary and shows "Exit node <name>"
under the instructions and script; the remove question names it too.
RepoEnvBadge shows a route icon for it, and its popover says internet
traffic and the clone go through it. GET /v1/repo-env and
GET /v1/repo-env/names return exit_node; an older server leaves it out,
which reads as none.
Base: omnigent v0.18.0 (a8676d23e88d), after 0001-0034.
